On May 28, 1:59 pm, Janis <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
> On 28 Mai, 12:13, James <james.ka...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Skilled in attack spells is also useful, especially if you're
> > wearing a robe.

> Sure, it's useful; using spells is useful for quite every class.
> Especially in the rare cases where you happen to have got a robe.

There are at least two ways you can easily get a robe.  It's
rare for me to get beyond the castle without one.  (But there
are always trade offs involved.  A robe means GDSM, which means
that reflection must come from somewhere else.  And of course, a
Samurai doesn't want a shield, so choosing a robe ends up
meaning no amulet of life saving.)

And I consider attack spells almost essential for disenchanters,
and any monster you don't want to get too close to on watery
levels.

> The Samurai may like to spend few XLs to become Skilled in attack
> spells; I'll add that to the "fun" argument. In my own games it
> very much depends whether I try to read spellbooks at all; it's
> generally too risky for many character classes (unless you take
> extreem precaucions, like pre-identifying or blessing a spellbook
> and reading it on a safe spot on a no-teleport level and having
> the respective stats pushed to increase the chances to read it).

I don't consider price-id'ing the spell books an extreme
precaution, and I'll also take A little bit of risk at times.
But a high XL reduces the risk as well; at XL30, you're almost
certainly guaranteed to be able to read them all, as long as
they aren't cursed (and your pet will tell you that).  Another
argument for uping your experience.

> > If keeping your XL down would guarantee no Liches, I could
> > see it, but I don't think that's the case.

> Yes, you've guaranteed liches in the Castle, and just
> genociding them seems to be the most simple counteraction
> (modulo conducts), surely more effective than keeping XL low.

Which supposes a scroll of genocide.  They seem to be a lot
rarer than robes in my games.  (In most games, a priest, plus a
pet, plus a polymorph trap results in a robe.)

And the Liches in the Castle worry me less than the ones that
pop up later on; you know they're there, and you can prepare for
them.  It's the ones that are suddenly created, later, right
next to you, that bother me the most.  If keeping my XL down
would guarantee no Liches other than in the castle, it might
still be worth it.

> > Once you're past the castle, got the important resistences
> > and your final armor (and so have an AC of -25 or less, the
> > only really worrisome monsters are those you're going to see
> > anyway.

> That is probably true. But if a Samurai is so buff as you say
> here, why quaff potions of gain level at all? Spells are yet
> more unnecessary in this scenario, and the potions of better
> use.

I agree that it's generally better to keep the potions of gain
level.  Not because it's a bad idea to increase your level,
however, but because there are less costly ways to do so.  I'm
normally well over XL/20 once I get below the Valley of the
Dead, without quaffing.

(In the meantime, my current Rogue has already marked the
vibrating square, with the candelabra, candles applied, and the
bell, but has no unholy water, and the only potion of gain level
I've seen that was cursed was used on the quest.  And I really
don't want to go through the final level the hard way.)

